    # The STAGED blob, never the working tree. The gate's whole claim is "the version
    # being committed passes its own selftest", and running the working-tree copy tests
    # a version that may not be the one entering history. Constructed and confirmed:
    # stage a broken version, revert the working tree without staging, and the hook is
    # green while the broken version lands (Codex found it, de reproduced it,
    # 2026-09-01). The manifest half of this same hook already argues exactly this at
    # the `git show :scripts/pod_drift.py` below -- the rule was stated and then not
    # applied twelve lines above it.
    #
    # scripts/harness.py used to be exempt here, on the grounds that `harness check` is
    # its selftest. It is not: `check` runs run_checks(), while `--selftest` is what
    # verifies every check FAILs on its broken world. The core proof of the whole
    # harness has never run at commit time. It runs now, and it is slower than the rest
    # of this gate combined, which is the honest cost of actually checking.
    # The staged blob runs from a sibling path NEXT TO the real file. Two earlier
    # placements failed, and both for the same reason: a file's ROOT is
    # __file__-relative, so wherever the copy sits is what ROOT means to it.
    # repo_root()/.git raised NotADirectoryError in every linked worktree (.git is a
    # 60-byte file there) and blocked five sessions from committing; the
    # `git rev-parse --git-path hook-staged` tree that replaced it resolved, but every
    # path the file derived -- imports AND data -- then pointed into that empty tree.
    # PYTHONPATH can patch the imports; it cannot patch a data path, and
    # datagen/build_corpus.py resolves filters/pass1_garbage.py from ROOT and died on
    # FileNotFoundError. A red that says nothing about the commit is the same
    # false-alarm class as a green that says nothing: both make the gate unreadable.
    # Beside the real file, ROOT is the repo, and only the file under test differs.
    # try/finally, not a line after the loop: these copies now live in the WORKING TREE,
    # so a refusal that returns early would leave a .hookstaged_* file behind for the
    # next session to find dirty. The parallel tree could be left to rot; this cannot.
    staged_copies = []
    # Files whose exemption is partial: run the flag that names the assertions which DO
    # work here, instead of skipping the file entirely.
    PARTIAL = {f: v[1] for f, v in NEEDS_DATA.items() if isinstance(v, tuple)}
    # Files that run fully but whose pass/fail verdict is not on the default flag. Kept
    # separate from PARTIAL: that map means "exempt, run the subset that works here",
    # and its entries are NEEDS_DATA rows. This one means "runs everything, but say
    # which flag makes a failure fatal" -- putting scripts/test_recipe_required.py in
    # PARTIAL would have recorded it as data-exempt, which is false and would mislead
    # the next reader of NEEDS_DATA.
    SELFTEST_FLAG = {"scripts/test_recipe_required.py": "--strict",
                     # POSITIONAL, not a flag. file_claim.py (3d65ec86) takes an argparse
                     # SUBCOMMAND -- {acquire,release,release-all,status,selftest} with
                     # required=True -- so `--selftest` makes argparse exit 2 on a missing
                     # positional and the hook reads that as the selftest FAILING. It blocked
                     # every commit in every worktree, including merges, within minutes of
                     # landing. The file is fine: `python3 scripts/file_claim.py selftest`
                     # prints PASS (6 worlds). test_score_exit.py's comment at :706 records
                     # the tolerance this relies on -- a script that IGNORES an unknown
                     # argument needs no entry, and one that PARSES argv strictly needs one
                     # here. file_claim is the first strict parser in the map.
                     "scripts/file_claim.py": "selftest"}
    # A TEST ALSO RUNS WHEN ITS SUBJECT IS STAGED, not only when the test file itself is.
    #
    # MEASURED 2026-09-04, by staging a real defect and running this hook: dropping `demo_lang`
    # from eval/l1_fewshot.py's artifact name -- which collides the zh and en cells of the 2x2 onto
    # one file, the shape be.l1_3shot_retracted records -- committed GREEN with `selftests 0.07s`,
    # i.e. zero selftests ran. eval/test_l1_fewshot_2x2.py holds 13 checks that go red on exactly
    # that edit and is correctly listed in SELFTEST_FILES, but the loop intersects the map with the
    # STAGED set, so a test only runs when someone edits the test. The file under test is the file
    # that changes.
    #
    # Keyed on the subject, listing the tests that read it. DERIVED, not guessed (6e's item,
    # 2026-09-04): runs/audit_0904/derive_subject_tests.py reads every test's imports and string
    # literals with ast, and runs/audit_0904/subject_test_budget.py picks pairs deepest-first
    # under a per-subject wall-time budget. Both are committed so this table can be re-derived
    # instead of trusted. Three measurements decided its shape:
    #
    #   - The loose "carries --selftest" set calls 167 files tests, including tools like
    #     scripts/profile_step_cost.py and eval/cache_guard.py, which merely import their
    #     "subject". test_*.py is the honest set: 56 runnable pairs, not 146.
    #   - AN IMPORT EDGE IS NOT EVIDENCE A TEST EXERCISES ITS SUBJECT. 23 hook-runnable tests
    #     import train.py and nearly all of them want `Cfg`, where every shape constant lives.
    #     Ranking by names-taken and uses-of-those-names cuts train.py from 23 tests to 6.
    #   - Cost is the binding constraint. All 27 candidates measured here: 186.8s total, and an
    #     unbudgeted train.py commit would pay 126.1s. test_cursor_save alone is 51.3s.
    #
    # 45s per subject buys the table below: 27 pairs, 15 subjects, worst case train.py at 42.6s.
    # FIVE PAIRS DO NOT FIT AND ARE NAMED HERE rather than silently absent, because a hook that
    # quietly skips coverage reads as "covered everything": train.py -> test_cursor_save (51.3s,
    # 5 uses), test_num_id_resolve (16.8s, 3), test_muon_shape_lr (15.3s, 8); model.py ->
    # test_untie_head (14.2s, 3), test_zero_init_out (9.6s, 3). They stay in SELFTEST_FILES, so
    # editing one still runs it; what is missing is the reverse direction for those five.
    #
    # Only pairs where the test genuinely exercises the subject go here -- a test listed against
    # a file it does not read costs seconds on every commit and buys nothing.
    TESTS_FOR_SUBJECT = {
        "eval/l1_fewshot.py": {"eval/test_l1_fewshot_2x2.py", "eval/score_matrix.py",
                               "scripts/test_fewshot_demos.py", "scripts/test_fewshot_stop.py"},
        # score_matrix's own selftest loads l1_fewshot by path and asserts on artifact_path,
        # verify_artifact and the transcriber, so a change to either file can break it.
        "eval/score_matrix.py": {"eval/test_l1_fewshot_2x2.py"},
        "eval/l1_2x2_diagnose.py": {"eval/test_l1_fewshot_2x2.py"},
        # Derived, 2026-09-04. The per-subject cost in seconds is in the comment beside each.
        # test_mem_defaults_frozen guards BOTH train.py and model.py because the memory arm's
        # behaviour is split across them: the selection lives in model.ProductKeyMemory.forward
        # and the optimizer grouping in train.build_optimizers, and b0's two flags
        # (--mem_sel_lr, --mem_query_norm) each move one of the two.
        "train.py": {"scripts/test_arch_compat.py", "scripts/test_plan_length.py",
                     "scripts/test_untie_head.py",
                     "scripts/test_mem_defaults_frozen.py"},                 # 42.6s
        "model.py": {"scripts/test_arch_compat.py", "scripts/test_split_bitwise.py",
                     "scripts/test_value_embed.py",
                     "scripts/test_mem_defaults_frozen.py"},                 # 36.3s
        "scripts/loader.py": {"scripts/test_arch_compat.py", "scripts/test_sft_pack.py",
                              "scripts/test_eval_base_prompt_format.py",
                              "scripts/test_serve_history.py"},              # 22.0s
        "fone.py": {"scripts/test_arch_compat.py"},                          # 18.0s
        "scripts/card_claim.py": {"scripts/test_launch_claims.py"},          # 4.0s
        "eval/prefix_mask.py": {"scripts/test_sft_prefix.py"},               # 1.9s
        "datagen/prepare_sft.py": {"scripts/test_sft_pack.py"},              # 1.8s
        "scripts/harness.py": {"scripts/test_cvd_pattern.py", "scripts/test_drop_zombies.py",
                               "scripts/test_eval_registry.py", "scripts/test_pod_ps_judge.py",
                               "scripts/test_spawned_fast.py",
                               # The reverse direction of the dead-world scan: harness.py IS the
                               # file whose 88 worlds it judges, so an edit that moves a marker
                               # literal is exactly what it reports. 0.9s.
                               "runs/audit_0904/dead_worlds.py"},   # 2.0s
        "scripts/ledger_audit.py": {"scripts/test_ledger_predicates.py"},    # 0.6s
        "scripts/e1_29_floor_by_class.py": {"scripts/test_e1_29_floor_by_class.py"},
        "datagen/near_dedup_postpass.py": {"datagen/test_near_dedup_known.py"},
        "algorithms/rlvr_reward.py": {"algorithms/test_rlvr_reward_suite.py"},
        "scripts/e1_28_leak_scan.py": {"scripts/test_e1_28_leak_scan.py"},
        "scripts/e1_28_matched.py": {"scripts/test_e1_28_matched.py"},
    }
    _subject_tests = set()
    for _subj in staged("AM"):
        _subject_tests |= TESTS_FOR_SUBJECT.get(_subj, set())
    # Restricted to the map: a test pulled in this way still has to be one the hook can run here,
    # or a NEEDS_DATA file would be launched on a box without its data and fail for the wrong
    # reason. Its own staged-ness is irrelevant, so `git show :<f>` cannot be used for those --
    # handled below.
    _subject_tests &= (SELFTEST_FILES | set(PARTIAL))

    # Measured per selftest, not once around the loop, so the refusal can name WHICH
    # one did it (e1-14). A single before/after pair would say "something changed".
    state_before = _shared_repo_state(repo_root())
    # One temp root for every selftest this hook runs, removed in the same finally as the
    # staged copies. 19 of the tracked selftests mkdtemp without rmtree, and most cannot
    # clean up: the world is handed to an assertion that reads it after the function
    # returns. Passing TMPDIR down is the producer-side fix that needs no edit in any of
    # them. MEASURED 2026-09-05: 12 dirs and 7.5 MB from harness.py --selftest alone, on a
    # laptop whose disk the user has cleared three times. tempfile directly, not
    # scripts/tmpworld.py: the hook runs as a staged copy from the gitdir, where scripts/
    # is not importable.
    st_tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="aupai_hook_st_")
    try:
        for f in sorted(((SELFTEST_FILES | set(PARTIAL)) & set(staged("AM"))) | _subject_tests):
            # A staged file is read from the INDEX (the version being committed); a test pulled in
            # because its subject changed is read from the WORKING TREE, since it is not staged and
            # `git show :<f>` would fail or return an older blob. Both are the version that will be
            # in effect after this commit.
            if f in set(staged("AM")):
                blob = subprocess.run(["git", "show", f":{f}"], capture_output=True)
                if blob.returncode != 0:
                    print(f"REFUSING: cannot read staged {f}", file=sys.stderr)
                    sys.exit(1)
                content = blob.stdout
            else:
                # A subject-pulled test that is ABSENT is SKIPPED, not a refusal, and the hook says
                # so. The refusal I first wrote here deadlocked a worktree within hours, and it was
                # my own: .git/hooks/pre-commit is a symlink into the INTEGRATION tree, so every
                # worktree runs main's copy of this file while its own tree may be dozens of commits
                # behind. main's map named runs/audit_0904/dead_worlds.py, which exists on main and
                # not in a 58-behind worktree -- so committing refused for a missing file, and the
                # merge that would have supplied it refused because the tree was dirty. Neither
                # could go first.
                #
                # A missing file is not evidence about the staged change, and this branch cannot
                # tell "the test was deleted" from "this tree predates it". So: warn, skip, and let
                # behind-main (which fires before this and names the real remedy) be the check that
                # cares about the tree being old.
                try:
                    with open(os.path.join(repo_root(), f), "rb") as fh:
                        content = fh.read()
                except OSError as e:
                    print(f"hook: skipping {f} ({e.strerror}); it guards a staged file but is not "
                          f"in this tree -- merge main if that is unexpected", file=sys.stderr)
                    continue
            d, base = os.path.split(os.path.join(repo_root(), f))
            dst = os.path.join(d, f".hookstaged_{base}")
            staged_copies.append(dst)
            with open(dst, "wb") as fh:
                fh.write(content)
            flag = PARTIAL.get(f) or SELFTEST_FLAG.get(f, "--selftest")
            _env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if not k.startswith("GIT_")}
            _env["TMPDIR"] = _env["TEMP"] = _env["TMP"] = st_tmp
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, dst, flag], capture_output=True,
                               text=True, cwd=repo_root(), env=_env)
            if r.returncode != 0:
                tail = (r.stdout + r.stderr).strip().splitlines()[-6:]
                _origin = ("staged version" if f in set(staged("AM"))
                           else "working tree; it guards a staged file")
                print(f"REFUSING: {f} {flag} failed ({_origin}):\n" + "\n".join(tail),
                      file=sys.stderr)
                sys.exit(1)
            # A selftest that PASSED can still have written to the shared repo, and that
            # is the worse case: rc=0 reads as "verified" while another session's git is
            # already broken. Checked after the rc check so a crashing selftest reports
            # its own error first.
            after = _shared_repo_state(repo_root())
            if after != state_before:
                what = []
                if after[0] != state_before[0]:
                    what.append(f".git/config changed ({state_before[0]} -> {after[0]})")
                gained, lost = after[1] - state_before[1], state_before[1] - after[1]
                if gained:
                    what.append(f"staged {sorted(gained)[:4]}")
                if lost:
                    what.append(f"unstaged {sorted(lost)[:4]}")
                print(f"REFUSING: {f} --selftest changed the SHARED repo: "
                      + "; ".join(what) + ".\n"
                      "It passed, which makes this worse than a failure: rc=0 reads as "
                      "verified while every other session's git is already broken.\n"
                      "A selftest must build its world from nothing the caller is "
                      "holding -- strip GIT_* from every subprocess it spawns "
                      "(pod_drift.py:61-66).",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                sys.exit(1)
    finally:
        for p in staged_copies:
            if os.path.exists(p):
                os.unlink(p)
        shutil.rmtree(st_tmp, ignore_errors=True)
    print(f"hook: selftests {time.time() - t0:.2f}s", file=sys.stderr)

    # Harness check: the full invariant suite. ~5s locally; GPU/pod checks SKIP
    # on dev machines. CI is the second line; the commit is the first.
    t0 = time.time()
    repo = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"], capture_output=True, text=True
    ).stdout.strip()
    if repo and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(repo, "scripts", "harness.py")):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(
                [sys.executable, os.path.join(repo, "scripts", "harness.py"), "check"],
                capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=180,
            )
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            print("REFUSING: harness check did not finish in 180s -- rerun it by hand; "
                  "a hook that hangs is a hook people bypass", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(1)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            # stdout AND stderr, and the FAIL lines pulled out. Printing stdout alone
            # produced "REFUSING: harness check failed:" with an empty reason when the
            # run crashed or a check raised, because those write to stderr (fb,
            # 2026-08-31). A refusal that does not say what failed cannot be acted on.
            failed = [ln for ln in r.stdout.splitlines() if "[FAIL]" in ln]
            print("REFUSING: harness check failed", file=sys.stderr)
            for ln in failed:
                print(f"  {ln.strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
            if not failed:
                print("  (no [FAIL] line -- the check run itself failed)", file=sys.stderr)
                print(f"  exit {r.returncode}", file=sys.stderr)
                tail = (r.stderr or r.stdout or "").strip().splitlines()[-15:]
                for ln in tail:
                    print(f"  {ln}", file=sys.stderr)
            # A ledger another session is mid-write reads as malformed for a moment.
            # Say so rather than leaving the reader to guess at a transient.
            print("  if this passes when rerun by hand, a concurrent write raced a "
                  "ledger read -- report it, do not --no-verify", file=sys.stderr)
            sys.exit(1)
        # TIMEOUT lines print on a PASSING run too. A check that hit its deadline did
        # not run, and the hook is where anyone would notice: while a timeout reported
        # SKIP, the hook printed nothing and `check` exited 0, so a check that timed out
        # on every run was a permanent silent pass (44, D5). The commit still proceeds --
        # one slow run is not a defect -- but the reader is told which check went unrun.
        for ln in r.stdout.splitlines():
            if "[TIME]" in ln:
                print(f"  TIMED OUT (did not run): {ln.strip()}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"hook: harness check {time.time() - t0:.2f}s", file=sys.stderr)

    # scripts/harness.py is DELIBERATELY not in SELFTEST_FILES, and this block is the reason
    # recorded rather than the absence left to be read as an omission (de, on 6e's C5 ruling
    # 2026-09-04). `harness check --selftest` builds a broken world for each of 79 checks and
    # takes ~4 min -- a hook that slow is a hook people --no-verify past, which buys less than
    # the exclusion costs. What made the exclusion a defect was silence: two commits changing
    # check code printed `selftests 0.04s` and `34.20s`, and neither number included a single
    # one of the 79. A timing line cannot tell you the run was empty.
    #
    # The substitute is scoped by FUNCTION, not by file: every CHECKS function lives in
    # harness.py, so a file-level filter selects all 79 and saves nothing (measured). This maps
    # the staged diff's changed line numbers to the enclosing `def` and verifies only the checks
    # whose run() or broken() actually changed -- 1 of 79 in 6.7s on the commit that added it.
    #
    # NOT A REPLACEMENT, and it says so in its own output: an edit to a shared helper or to the
    # CHECKS table changes no check function, selects nothing, and is reported as "no answer".
    # The full run stays the coverage claim; this only catches the case where someone edits a
    # check and its world stops discriminating. That case is not hypothetical -- it is how
    # _broken_run_commits_resolve was caught planting its defect into a superseded ledger row.
    if "scripts/harness.py" in staged("AM") and repo:
        t0 = time.time()
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(
                [sys.executable, os.path.join(repo, "scripts", "harness.py"), "check",
                 "--selftest-touching", "scripts/harness.py"],
                capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=240,
            )
            out = (r.stdout + r.stderr).strip()
            tail = [ln for ln in out.splitlines() if ln.strip()][-6:]
            if r.returncode != 0:
                print("REFUSING: a changed check does not FAIL on its broken world",
                      file=sys.stderr)
                for ln in tail:
                    print(f"  {ln}", file=sys.stderr)
                sys.exit(1)
            for ln in tail[-1:]:
                print(f"  {ln}", file=sys.stderr)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            print("  scoped harness selftest did not finish in 240s -- run "
                  "`harness check --selftest` by hand", file=sys.stderr)
        print(f"hook: harness selftest (scoped) {time.time() - t0:.2f}s", file=sys.stderr)

    # THE MANIFEST REGEN LIVED HERE AND IS GONE (shape A, 6e ruling 2026-09-04).
    # data/pod_head_manifest.txt is no longer tracked: scripts/pod_push.sh generates it from
    # the HEAD it ships and ships it last. Regenerating it on every commit is what made it
    # the top friction cause -- 4 of 15 rows in runs/friction.jsonl -- because any two
    # branches that both committed collided on it. Worse, MEASURED 2026-09-04: in a merge
    # commit this ran AFTER git had already built the tree, so the regenerated bytes landed
    # in the index and never in the commit, leaving the file permanently dirty against HEAD
    # and aborting the NEXT merge; and at 81f091af a clean textual merge spliced one side's
    # manifest line with the other side's file content, so main asserted d07a474f for a
    # roadmap whose tree held 8dc68958 (1 stale of 423 entries).
    print(f"hook: total {time.time() - t_start:.2f}s", file=sys.stderr)


def _selftest():
    """Prove the integration-tree guard both fires and lets a merge through.

    Runs with every GIT_* variable removed from the environment first. git exports
    GIT_DIR (on merge) and GIT_INDEX_FILE to hooks, and a `git init` in a temp
    directory that inherits GIT_DIR re-initialises the SHARED repo instead: core.bare
    flipped to true and the fixture f.txt landed in the real index, twice on
    2026-09-02 (02:49Z and 04:4xZ), taking every session's git down until someone
    unset it. pod_drift.py:61 recorded the mechanism a day earlier, inside one script.

    Two worlds, and the second is the one that mattered: on 2026-09-02 `git merge
    --no-edit e1` on main was REFUSED and left a half-finished merge in the shared
    tree, because git calls this script as pre-merge-commit BEFORE writing
    MERGE_HEAD. Testing only "a plain commit on main is refused" passes on the
    broken version, which is why that version shipped.

    Runs the real hook as a subprocess in a temp repo whose path is declared the
    integration tree, so the guard is live rather than simulated.

    EVERY git call here strips the caller's git environment. The hook runs with GIT_DIR
    set to the committing worktree's gitdir, and `git init` in a fresh directory with
    GIT_DIR inherited reconfigures the SHARED repo instead -- it sets core.bare=true,
    which breaks every git command for every session until someone unsets it. I did
    exactly that at 02:49Z on 2026-09-02 and blocked main for two minutes; the mechanism
    was already written down in pod_drift.py:61-66 and I did not apply it. Reproduced
    since: GIT_DIR must point at a WORKTREE gitdir (.git/worktrees/<name>) for the bare
    flag to be set -- a plain repo's GIT_DIR does not do it, which is why a casual test
    of this looks safe, and why the destructive form only appears from a worktree, where
    every session except the controller works.
    """
    for k in [k for k in os.environ if k.startswith("GIT_")]:
        os.environ.pop(k)

    hook = os.path.realpath(__file__)
    fails = []
    # os.environ is already GIT_*-free four lines up, so `clean` is it. Named anyway,
    # because every git call below reads as deliberate rather than as inheriting
    # whatever the caller held -- and the seven-name list this replaced was narrower
    # than the prefix sweep for no reason.
    clean = dict(os.environ)

    def run(cwd, argv0, env_extra):
        dst = os.path.join(cwd, argv0)
        shutil.copyfile(hook, dst)
        os.chmod(dst, 0o755)
        env = dict(clean, AUPAI_INTEGRATION_TREE=cwd)
        env.pop("AUPAI_CONTROLLER", None)
        env.update(env_extra)
        r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, dst], cwd=cwd, capture_output=True,
                           text=True, env=env)
        return r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr

    d = tempfile.mkdtemp()
    try:
        for a in (["init", "-q", "-b", "main", "."], ["config", "user.email", "t@t"],
                  ["config", "user.name", "t"]):
            subprocess.run(["git"] + a, cwd=d, capture_output=True, env=clean)
        with open(os.path.join(d, "f.txt"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("base\n")
        subprocess.run(["git", "add", "f.txt"], cwd=d, capture_output=True, env=clean)
        subprocess.run(["git", "commit", "-q", "-m", "base"], cwd=d, capture_output=True,
                       env=clean)

        # WORLD 1 (must REFUSE): an ordinary commit in the integration tree on main.
        rc, out = run(d, "pre-commit", {})
        if rc == 0 or "REFUSING: this is the integration tree" not in out:
            fails.append(f"world 1: a plain commit on main was NOT refused (rc={rc})")

        # WORLD 2 (must PASS): the same script invoked the way `git merge` invokes it.
        # No MERGE_HEAD on disk -- that is exactly the state the old code got wrong.
        if os.path.exists(os.path.join(d, ".git", "MERGE_HEAD")):
            fails.append("world 2 precondition: MERGE_HEAD exists, so it proves nothing")
        rc, out = run(d, "pre-merge-commit", {})
        if "REFUSING: this is the integration tree" in out:
            fails.append("world 2: a merge was refused in the integration tree "
                         "(pre-merge-commit with no MERGE_HEAD yet)")

        # WORLD 3 (must PASS): finishing a CONFLICTED merge, where MERGE_HEAD is on disk.
        with open(os.path.join(d, ".git", "MERGE_HEAD"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("0" * 40 + "\n")
        rc, out = run(d, "pre-commit", {})
        if "REFUSING: this is the integration tree" in out:
            fails.append("world 3: the commit that resolves a conflicted merge was refused")
        os.unlink(os.path.join(d, ".git", "MERGE_HEAD"))

        # WORLD 4 (must PASS): reflog action says merge, name and MERGE_HEAD say nothing.
        rc, out = run(d, "pre-commit", {"GIT_REFLOG_ACTION": "merge e1"})
        if "REFUSING: this is the integration tree" in out:
            fails.append("world 4: GIT_REFLOG_ACTION=merge was refused")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(d, ignore_errors=True)

    # WORLD 5 (must PASS): the OTHER gate. A real linked worktree, diverged from main,
    # merging main in -- which is what the behind-main refusal tells you to do. Needs a
    # true merge, not a fast-forward: a fast-forward runs no hook, which is how this
    # stayed hidden. Kept separate because it needs a worktree, not just a repo.
    d2 = tempfile.mkdtemp()
    try:
        def git(*a, cwd=d2):
            return subprocess.run(["git"] + list(a), cwd=cwd, capture_output=True,
                                  text=True, env=clean)
        for a in (("init", "-q", "-b", "main", "."), ("config", "user.email", "t@t"),
                  ("config", "user.name", "t")):
            git(*a)
        with open(os.path.join(d2, "f.txt"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("base\n")
        git("add", "f.txt")
        git("commit", "-q", "-m", "base")
        git("branch", "-q", "feat")
        with open(os.path.join(d2, "m.txt"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("m\n")
        git("add", "m.txt")
        git("commit", "-q", "-m", "main-ahead")
        wt = os.path.join(d2, "wt")
        git("worktree", "add", "-q", wt, "feat")
        hooks = os.path.join(d2, ".git", "hooks")
        os.makedirs(hooks, exist_ok=True)
        for name in ("pre-commit", "pre-merge-commit"):
            dst = os.path.join(hooks, name)
            if os.path.exists(dst):
                os.unlink(dst)
            shutil.copyfile(hook, dst)
            os.chmod(dst, 0o755)
        with open(os.path.join(wt, "d.txt"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("d\n")
        git("add", "d.txt", cwd=wt)
        env = dict(clean, AUPAI_BEHIND_MAIN_OK="1")
        subprocess.run(["git", "commit", "-q", "-m", "feat"], cwd=wt,
                       capture_output=True, env=env)
        behind = git("rev-list", "--count", "HEAD..main", cwd=wt).stdout.strip()
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "merge", "--no-edit", "main"], cwd=wt,
                           capture_output=True, text=True,
                           env={k: v for k, v in clean.items()
                                if k != "AUPAI_BEHIND_MAIN_OK"})
        out = r.stdout + r.stderr
        if behind == "0":
            fails.append("world 5 precondition: worktree is not behind, proves nothing")
        if "REFUSING: this tree is" in out:
            fails.append("world 5: `git merge --no-edit main` was refused in a worktree "
                         f"{behind} behind main -- the command the refusal itself prints")

        # WORLDS 5b/5c: the append-only-ledger exemption, BOTH directions, in the same
        # worktree. 5b is why it exists -- `harness friction add --commit` appended a row and
        # the hook refused the commit, leaving the ledger dirty, twice in one hour; the second
        # time the tree fell behind BETWEEN a clean merge and the commit, so "merge first" was
        # not available. 5c is the load-bearing half: one code path alongside the ledger and
        # the refusal must stand, or the exemption is a way to commit code from behind main by
        # attaching a ledger row.
        #
        # Re-diverge first: world 5 merged main in, so the worktree is no longer behind and a
        # PASS here would prove nothing about the exemption.
        for a in (("checkout", "-q", "-b", "feat2"),):
            git(*a, cwd=wt)
        with open(os.path.join(d2, "m2.txt"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("m2\n")
        git("add", "m2.txt")
        git("commit", "-q", "-m", "main-ahead-2")
        behind2 = git("rev-list", "--count", "HEAD..main", cwd=wt).stdout.strip()
        if behind2 == "0":
            fails.append("world 5b precondition: worktree is not behind again, proves nothing")
        # The ledger must be union-merged where the hook looks, which is .gitattributes in the
        # tree being committed -- copied from the real one so the fixture cannot disagree with
        # the repo about which paths are ledgers.
        with open(os.path.join(wt, ".gitattributes"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("runs/friction.jsonl merge=union\n")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(wt, "runs"), exist_ok=True)
        git("add", ".gitattributes", cwd=wt)
        subprocess.run(["git", "commit", "-q", "-m", "attrs"], cwd=wt, capture_output=True,
                       env=dict(clean, AUPAI_BEHIND_MAIN_OK="1"))
        with open(os.path.join(wt, "runs", "friction.jsonl"), "a") as fh:
            fh.write('{"id": "x", "kind": "merge"}\n')
        git("add", "runs/friction.jsonl", cwd=wt)
        env_no_flag = {k: v for k, v in clean.items() if k != "AUPAI_BEHIND_MAIN_OK"}
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "commit", "-q", "-m", "friction row"], cwd=wt,
                           capture_output=True, text=True, env=env_no_flag)
        out5b = r.stdout + r.stderr
        if "REFUSING: this tree is" in out5b:
            fails.append(f"world 5b: a commit staging ONLY runs/friction.jsonl was refused in "
                         f"a worktree {behind2} behind main -- the friction row is the write "
                         f"that must never be refused, and it needs no flag")

        with open(os.path.join(wt, "runs", "friction.jsonl"), "a") as fh:
            fh.write('{"id": "y", "kind": "hook"}\n')
        with open(os.path.join(wt, "code.py"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("x = 1\n")
        git("add", "runs/friction.jsonl", "code.py", cwd=wt)
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "commit", "-q", "-m", "ledger plus code"], cwd=wt,
                           capture_output=True, text=True, env=env_no_flag)
        out5c = r.stdout + r.stderr
        if "REFUSING: this tree is" not in out5c:
            fails.append("world 5c: a commit staging code.py ALONGSIDE the ledger was NOT "
                         "refused -- the exemption must be narrow, or a ledger row becomes a "
                         "way to commit code from behind main")
        elif "code.py" not in out5c:
            fails.append(f"world 5c: refused without naming the path that is not a ledger, so "
                         f"the writer cannot see why: {out5c[-120:]}")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(d2, ignore_errors=True)

    # WORLD 6 (must DETECT): _shared_repo_state notices a selftest that wrote to the
    # shared repo, which is the e1-14 detector. Built from the real leak: a fake
    # selftest that runs `git init` with GIT_DIR pointing at a WORKTREE gitdir, the
    # only form that flips core.bare. Everything happens inside a throwaway repo --
    # reproducing a destructive defect against the real one is how it got loose twice.
    d3 = tempfile.mkdtemp()
    try:
        def g3(*a, cwd=d3):
            return subprocess.run(["git"] + list(a), cwd=cwd, capture_output=True,
                                  text=True, env=clean)
        for a in (("init", "-q", "-b", "main", "."), ("config", "user.email", "t@t"),
                  ("config", "user.name", "t")):
            g3(*a)
        with open(os.path.join(d3, "f.txt"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("base\n")
        g3("add", "f.txt")
        g3("commit", "-q", "-m", "base")
        g3("branch", "-q", "side")
        wt3 = os.path.join(d3, "wt")
        g3("worktree", "add", "-q", wt3, "side")
        gd = subprocess.run(["git", "rev-parse", "--absolute-git-dir"], cwd=wt3,
                            capture_output=True, text=True, env=clean).stdout.strip()

        before = _shared_repo_state(d3)
        if not before[0]:
            fails.append("world 6 precondition: no config digest, so a change cannot show")
        cfg_path = os.path.join(
            subprocess.run(["git", "-C", d3, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute",
                            "--git-common-dir"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           env=clean).stdout.strip(), "config")
        with open(cfg_path, "rb") as fh:
            cfg_bytes = fh.read()
        victim = tempfile.mkdtemp()
        try:
            subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", victim], capture_output=True,
                           env=dict(clean, GIT_DIR=gd))
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(victim, ignore_errors=True)
        after = _shared_repo_state(d3)
        if after == before:
            fails.append("world 6: the leak did not reproduce, so the detector is "
                         "untested -- GIT_DIR must point at a worktree gitdir")
        elif subprocess.run(["git", "-C", d3, "config", "--get", "core.bare"],
                            capture_output=True, text=True,
                            env=clean).stdout.strip() != "true":
            fails.append("world 6: config changed but core.bare is not true -- the "
                         "reproduction is not the leak we mean")
        # Restore the exact bytes, then confirm equality returns -- so a difference is
        # attributable to one selftest and not to drift. `git config --unset core.bare`
        # does NOT restore them: `git init` wrote `bare = false`, and the unset deletes
        # that line rather than putting it back, leaving a config that differs from the
        # baseline. A whole-file hash notices that; a core.bare comparison would not,
        # which is the point of hashing.
        with open(cfg_path, "wb") as fh:
            fh.write(cfg_bytes)
        if _shared_repo_state(d3) != before:
            fails.append("world 6: state did not return to baseline after restoring the "
                         "config bytes, so a difference cannot be attributed to one "
                         "selftest")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(d3, ignore_errors=True)

    # WORLDS 7-10 ARE GONE, and their absence is the point (shape A, 6e ruling 2026-09-04).
    # All four tested that the hook's manifest regeneration left no residue on a refusal:
    # world 7 a refusal, 8 the end-to-end livelock, 9 a deliberately staged manifest
    # surviving, 10 a gate added after the manifest stage. The hook no longer regenerates
    # anything -- data/pod_head_manifest.txt is untracked and pod_push.sh generates it from
    # the HEAD it ships -- so there is no residue to leave and nothing for those worlds to
    # measure. Deleting them rather than keeping them green on a code path that no longer
    # exists: a world that cannot fail certifies nothing, and four passing worlds guarding
    # deleted code would read as coverage.
    # WORLD 7 (must REFUSE): a MERGE that drops a row only the OTHER parent had. The
    # HEAD-vs-index comparison is blind to this by construction -- ours never had the row, so
    # nothing is missing relative to ours -- and 6e reported exactly that shape on
    # runs/experiments.jsonl. Both directions: the same merge keeping BOTH rows must pass, or
    # the world proves only that the hook refuses merges.
    d6 = tempfile.mkdtemp()
    try:
        def git6(*a, cwd=d6):
            return subprocess.run(["git"] + list(a), cwd=cwd, capture_output=True,
                                  text=True, env=clean)
        for a in (("init", "-q", "-b", "main", "."), ("config", "user.email", "t@t"),
                  ("config", "user.name", "t")):
            git6(*a)
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(d6, "runs"), exist_ok=True)
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(d6, "scripts"), exist_ok=True)
        # The REAL predicate, not a stub: copy ledger_audit.py so the world exercises the
        # same code the hook imports. A hand-written stub would agree with whatever the hook
        # does, which is the shape this file's own selftest notes warn about.
        # `hook` is this file's realpath, so scripts/ledger_audit.py sits two levels up from
        # scripts/hooks/pre-commit. Derived, not a constant: a hard-coded root would be the
        # wrong tree in a linked worktree.
        # BOTH FILES. ledger_audit imports test_ledger_predicates at module scope, and the
        # hook's ledger step is wrapped in `except ImportError: SKIP` -- so copying only
        # ledger_audit.py makes the step silently do nothing and the world reports the defect
        # as un-caught when the hook would have caught it. Measured while writing this world.
        _src_scripts = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(hook))
        for _dep in ("ledger_audit.py", "test_ledger_predicates.py"):
            shutil.copyfile(os.path.join(_src_scripts, _dep),
                            os.path.join(d6, "scripts", _dep))
        led = os.path.join(d6, "runs", "experiments.jsonl")
        base_rows = ['{"name":"r1","started":"2026-09-01 00:00","status":"ok"}']
        with open(led, "w") as fh:
            fh.write("\n".join(base_rows) + "\n")
        with open(os.path.join(d6, ".gitattributes"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("runs/experiments.jsonl merge=union\n")
        git6("add", "-A")
        git6("commit", "-q", "-m", "base")

        ours_row = '{"name":"ours","started":"2026-09-02 00:00","status":"ok"}'
        theirs_row = '{"name":"theirs","started":"2026-09-03 00:00","status":"ok"}'
        git6("checkout", "-qb", "theirs")
        with open(led, "a") as fh:
            fh.write(theirs_row + "\n")
        git6("commit", "-qam", "theirs")
        git6("checkout", "-q", "main")
        with open(led, "a") as fh:
            fh.write(ours_row + "\n")
        git6("commit", "-qam", "ours")

        hooks6 = os.path.join(d6, ".git", "hooks")
        os.makedirs(hooks6, exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copyfile(hook, os.path.join(hooks6, "pre-commit"))
        os.chmod(os.path.join(hooks6, "pre-commit"), 0o755)

        # POSITIVE FIRST: the honest union merge keeps both rows and must PASS. Without this
        # the negative below would pass on a hook that refuses every merge of a ledger.
        git6("merge", "--no-edit", "--no-commit", "theirs")
        r_ok = subprocess.run([os.path.join(hooks6, "pre-commit")], cwd=d6,
                              capture_output=True, text=True, env=clean)
        body = open(led).read()
        if ours_row not in body or theirs_row not in body:
            fails.append("world 7 precondition: the union merge did not keep both rows, so "
                         "the positive case tests nothing")
        elif r_ok.returncode != 0:
            fails.append("world 7 positive: the hook refused a merge that kept BOTH rows "
                         f"({(r_ok.stderr or '')[:120]})")

        # NEGATIVE: drop the row only THEIRS had, exactly what a hand resolution does.
        with open(led, "w") as fh:
            fh.write("\n".join(base_rows + [ours_row]) + "\n")
        git6("add", "runs/experiments.jsonl")
        r_bad = subprocess.run([os.path.join(hooks6, "pre-commit")], cwd=d6,
                               capture_output=True, text=True, env=clean)
        out6 = r_bad.stdout + r_bad.stderr
        if r_bad.returncode == 0:
            fails.append("world 7: a merge dropping a row only the OTHER parent had was "
                         "allowed -- the HEAD-vs-index comparison cannot see it and nothing "
                         "else looked")
        elif "theirs" not in out6:
            fails.append(f"world 7: refused but did not NAME the dropped row: {out6[:160]}")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(d6, ignore_errors=True)

    # WORLD 8 (must REFUSE): a staged DEFECT in a subject whose test lives in another file.
    # 6e's item, and the shape is measured rather than hypothesised: on 2026-09-04, dropping
    # `demo_lang` from eval/l1_fewshot.py's artifact name committed GREEN with `selftests 0.07s`,
    # because the loop intersected SELFTEST_FILES with the STAGED set and the test was not staged.
    # The file under test is the file that changes.
    #
    # BOTH DIRECTIONS, because a world that only checks the refusal would pass on a hook that
    # runs the test for every commit -- which is the failure mode of an unbudgeted map (a train.py
    # commit would pay 126.1s). So: staging a defect in a mapped subject must refuse, and staging
    # an UNMAPPED file must not run that subject's tests at all.
    #
    # THE SUBJECT MUST NOT ITSELF BE IN SELFTEST_FILES, AND MUST RUN STANDALONE. Two earlier
    # versions of this world were wrong in different ways and each said so rather than passing:
    #   - scripts/ledger_audit.py IS registered, so staging it ran its own selftest and the
    #     refusal came from the pre-existing loop, not the map ("refused but not by the mapped
    #     test").
    #   - datagen/near_dedup_postpass.py imports build_corpus, which imports holdout and loader,
    #     so a two-file world refused with ModuleNotFoundError. That refusal even NAMED the mapped
    #     test, so a name-matching assertion accepted it: the world was green for a missing import.
    # Hence the pair used now -- algorithms/rlvr_reward.py -> algorithms/test_rlvr_reward_suite.py:
    # the subject is unregistered so only the map reaches it, imports nothing outside the stdlib,
    # and the test is 0.12s. And the assertion below demands the test's OWN failure text, not its
    # filename, so an import error cannot satisfy it.
    d8 = tempfile.mkdtemp()
    try:
        def git8(*a):
            return subprocess.run(["git"] + list(a), cwd=d8, capture_output=True, text=True,
                                  env=clean)
        for a in (("init", "-q", "-b", "main", "."), ("config", "user.email", "t@t"),
                  ("config", "user.name", "t")):
            git8(*a)
        hooks8 = os.path.join(d8, ".git", "hooks")
        os.makedirs(hooks8, exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy(hook, os.path.join(hooks8, "pre-commit"))
        os.chmod(os.path.join(hooks8, "pre-commit"), 0o755)
        # WHERE THE REAL PAIR LIVES: git, not the hook's own path. `hook` is a realpath of
        # __file__, and when the hook is run as a STAGED COPY -- which is how it is verified at
        # commit time, from .hookstaged_pre-commit -- deriving the root by walking up from it
        # lands in the wrong directory, the pair is not copied, and world 8 fails with "a staged
        # defect was allowed" on a hook that works. Measured 2026-09-04 by running the copied
        # path: FAIL from the copy, ok from scripts/hooks/pre-commit, same bytes.
        _repo8 = repo_root() or os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(hook)))
        _copied = []
        for _rel in ("algorithms/rlvr_reward.py", "algorithms/test_rlvr_reward_suite.py"):
            _src = os.path.join(_repo8, _rel)
            if os.path.exists(_src):
                os.makedirs(os.path.join(d8, os.path.dirname(_rel)), exist_ok=True)
                shutil.copy(_src, os.path.join(d8, _rel))
                _copied.append(_rel)
        if len(_copied) != 2:
            fails.append(f"world 8: could not stage its pair from {_repo8} -- the world would "
                         f"pass without testing anything (copied {_copied})")
        git8("add", "-A")
        git8("commit", "-q", "-m", "base")

        # THE WORLD MUST BE GREEN BEFORE IT IS BROKEN, or a refusal proves nothing. Measured
        # directly: run the mapped test against the unmutated copy.
        _pre = subprocess.run([sys.executable, "algorithms/test_rlvr_reward_suite.py",
                               "--selftest"], cwd=d8, capture_output=True, text=True, env=clean)
        if _pre.returncode != 0:
            fails.append(f"world 8: the mapped test does not pass on the UNMUTATED world, so a "
                         f"refusal would not be caused by the mutation: "
                         f"{(_pre.stdout + _pre.stderr)[-200:]}")

        # NEGATIVE: break the SUBJECT, stage only the subject. Its test is not staged, so before
        # the map the hook ran nothing. `return 0.0` -> `return 1.0` on the no-boxed-answer path
        # makes every unparseable generation score as correct -- a real defect the suite's known
        # answers catch, and not a syntax error, which py_compile would catch first and let this
        # world pass for the wrong reason.
        #
        # THE GREEN RUN ABOVE LEFT A __pycache__, AND THIS MUTATION DOES NOT INVALIDATE IT.
        # Measured 2026-09-04 after it blocked b0 mid-merge: pyc invalidation is
        # (source mtime in WHOLE SECONDS, source size), `0.0` -> `1.0` is byte-for-byte the same
        # length (3565 -> 3565), so a mutation landing in the same wall-clock second as the green
        # run reuses the stale bytecode, the defect never executes, the inner hook exits 0, and
        # this world reports "a staged defect was allowed" on a hook that works. Six replica runs
        # gave rc 1,1,1,0,0,1 -- a race, which is why it never reproduced by hand and only bit
        # under a slow merge. Deleting the caches is the fix, not touching mtime: a
        # timestamp nudge assumes one second of granularity is enough and would come back.
        for _root8, _dirs8, _ in os.walk(d8):
            for _dn in list(_dirs8):
                if _dn == "__pycache__":
                    shutil.rmtree(os.path.join(_root8, _dn), ignore_errors=True)
                    _dirs8.remove(_dn)
        _rr = os.path.join(d8, "algorithms", "rlvr_reward.py")
        _src8 = open(_rr, encoding="utf-8").read() if os.path.exists(_rr) else ""
        _marker = "    pred = extract_boxed(gen_text)\n    if pred is None:\n        return 0.0"
        if _marker not in _src8:
            fails.append("world 8: rlvr_reward.reward_fn's no-answer branch is not where this "
                         "world mutates; the target moved and it would pass without testing "
                         "anything")
        else:
            with open(_rr, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                fh.write(_src8.replace(_marker, _marker[:-3] + "1.0", 1))
            git8("add", "algorithms/rlvr_reward.py")
            # THE MUTATION MUST BE LIVE BEFORE THE HOOK IS BLAMED. Without this the two
            # explanations of rc=0 -- "the hook did not run the mapped test" and "the mutation
            # never took effect" -- produce the same message, and the second one is what actually
            # happened on 2026-09-04 (see the pycache note above). Run the test directly against
            # the mutated copy: it must now FAIL.
            _mut = subprocess.run([sys.executable, "algorithms/test_rlvr_reward_suite.py"],
                                  cwd=d8, capture_output=True, text=True, env=clean)
            if _mut.returncode == 0:
                fails.append("world 8: the mapped test still PASSES on the mutated subject, so "
                             "this world can say nothing about the hook -- the defect did not "
                             "take effect (a stale __pycache__ does this: the mutation preserves "
                             "the file size and pyc invalidation is (mtime_seconds, size))")
            r8 = subprocess.run([os.path.join(hooks8, "pre-commit")], cwd=d8,
                                capture_output=True, text=True, env=clean)
            out8 = r8.stdout + r8.stderr
            if r8.returncode == 0:
                fails.append("world 8: a staged defect in a mapped SUBJECT was allowed -- its "
                             "test lives in another file and the loop only ran staged tests")
            elif "test_rlvr_reward_suite" not in out8:
                fails.append(f"world 8: refused but not by the mapped test: {out8[:200]}")
            elif not re.search(r"REFUSING:.*test_rlvr_reward_suite", out8):
                fails.append(f"world 8: the mapped test is named but is not what refused: "
                             f"{out8[:200]}")
            elif re.search(r"REFUSING:[^\n]*\n(?:[^\n]*\n){0,6}?[^\n]*"
                           r"(ModuleNotFoundError|ImportError)", out8):
                # NOT a bare `"ModuleNotFoundError" in out8`: the hook's own ledger step prints
                # `ledger audit SKIPPED: ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'ledger_audit'` in
                # any world that does not copy that module, so the bare test matched a line with
                # nothing to do with the refusal and this world reported FAIL while working
                # correctly. Scoped to the refusal's own output instead.
                fails.append(f"world 8: the refusal is an import failure, not the defect -- the "
                             f"world is incomplete and proves nothing: {out8[-200:]}")
            git8("checkout", "--", "algorithms/rlvr_reward.py")
            git8("reset", "-q")

        # POSITIVE: an UNMAPPED file must not pull that subject's tests in. Without this, a hook
        # that runs every test on every commit would pass the negative above.
        with open(os.path.join(d8, "unrelated.txt"), "w") as fh:
            fh.write("x\n")
        git8("add", "unrelated.txt")
        r8b = subprocess.run([os.path.join(hooks8, "pre-commit")], cwd=d8,
                             capture_output=True, text=True, env=clean)
        if "test_rlvr_reward_suite" in (r8b.stdout + r8b.stderr):
            fails.append("world 8 positive: an unmapped file ran a mapped subject's test -- the "
                         "map is not being consulted, every commit pays for every test")
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(d8, ignore_errors=True)

    for f in fails:
        print("SELFTEST FAIL: " + f, file=sys.stderr)
    # COUNTED FROM THE SOURCE, not a literal. The literal read "7 worlds" while nine were
    # running: 5b and 5c were added and the number was not, so the line under-reported the
    # coverage it was printed to describe -- the same shape as a selftests timing line that
    # includes zero files. Counts the `# WORLD <n>` markers, so a world added without a marker
    # is the only way to be miscounted now.
    _n = len({w for pair in re.findall(r"#\s*WORLDS?\s+([0-9]+[a-z]?)(?:/([0-9]+[a-z]?))?",
                                       open(__file__, encoding="utf-8").read())
              for w in pair if w})
    print("pre-commit selftest: %s (%d worlds)" % ("FAIL" if fails else "ok", _n),
          file=sys.stderr)
    return 1 if fails else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    if "--selftest" in sys.argv:
        sys.exit(_selftest())
    main()
